      "prompt": "I'm starting the Algorithms prep with dynamic programming. Get me a short overview of it from Wikipedia so I know where to begin.",
      "answer": "Wikipedia's summary is short: dynamic programming (DP) is both an optimization method and an algorithm design approach. Richard Bellman developed it in the 1950s ([source](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_programming)).\n\nHere's where I'd begin (my own suggestions, not from the summary):\n1. **Core ideas:** overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure.\n2. **Two approaches:** memoization (top-down) and tabulation (bottom-up).\n3. **Classic problems:** Fibonacci, then 0/1 knapsack, longest common subsequence (LCS) and edit distance.\n4. **Method:** for each problem, define the state, write the recurrence, set the base cases and choose the order to fill the table.\n\nOne topic per 25-minute pomodoro works well.",
